# Five classes answer to `stepped`: too many for arms: the call goes through the method table. Every row takes a number and gives one: the plan makes a selector beside the boxed one whose rows take and
# give it raw, and the walk names that selector where the argument is shown to be a number. Boxing the argument, unboxing it in the entry, boxing the result in the body and unboxing it in the caller
# were 30 of the 68 instructions of a turn.
# One row of `name` gives a text: a selector has one form for all its rows: every row of `name` boxes what it gives, and no entry boxes for its body.
# f1 = run, f2 = names
# RUN: walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# CHECK: number @Double = CallTable
# CHECK-NOT: @JSValue = CallTable
# RUN(NAMES): walk f2:Body mechanisms=all
# NAMES: @JSValue = CallTable
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: 1.9999990463256836 2.0000362396240234 123cap5123cap5
